Interop field notes

What the wire taught us bringing Weld up against CycloneDDS on Humble, Jazzy, and Rolling. Plain words first, then the term. Each section states the symptom, the proof, and the fix, so a future reader can re-verify instead of trusting us.

1. String padding is minimal, not fixed

Plain words: after a string, Cyclone and FastRTPS do not pad to a 4-byte boundary. They place the next field immediately and only pad as much as that field needs for its own alignment.

Background: XCDR1 lays bytes in order. Each primitive starts at an offset that is a multiple of its size. A string is a length plus characters plus a NUL terminator. The question is what happens between the NUL and the next field.

What we did wrong: our encoder always padded strings to a 4-byte boundary. For sensor_msgs/msg/Image with encoding mono8, we wrote 6 content bytes plus 2 pad bytes, then is_bigendian, then 3 more pad bytes, then step. Both vendors write no pad after NUL when a 1-byte member follows: NUL, then is_bigendian immediately, then 1 pad byte, then step.

Effect: every field from the small member onward shifted by 4 bytes in our samples. Cyclone read our is_bigendian slot as 0, our is_bigendian as its step, our step as its length, and then read past the buffer (zeros or heap). ros2 topic echo showed step: 0 and truncated garbage data for our images while ROS-to-ROS images decoded fine.

Proof, three independent checks:
- Bag capture: ros2 bag record stores raw CDR. Both a Cyclone-published and a FastRTPS-published Image show no string pad (mono8 NUL followed directly by the is_bigendian byte). Our encoder emitted 2 pad bytes there.
- Distinctive bytes: publishing is_bigendian=0xAA with step=0x04030201 made echo print step: 170 (0xAA), which is exactly our is_bigendian byte read as a 4-byte step. A distinctive marker removes all doubt about offsets.
- Weld-to-Weld loopback stayed byte-identical through the whole episode, which proves our side was self-consistent and the mismatch was against the vendors, not internal corruption.

Fix: internal/types/cdr.go no longer pads after string or wstring content. Each following member aligns itself with the existing Align call, and Bytes still pads the whole sample to 4 at the end. The decoder mirrors this: no pad skip after strings, next member aligns. Types without a small member after a string (the common case, including PointCloud2 whose string-like fields are followed by 4-byte members) encode byte-identically before and after, which is why PC2 passed throughout while Image failed.

Rule for contributors: pad is always relative to the next member's alignment, never unconditional to 4, except the trailing sample pad. If you add a codec path, test it with a small member directly after a 5-character string (the mono8 shape), which is the case that exposes eager padding.

2. DATA_FRAG body offset is 28, not 16

Plain words: a fragmented sample carries a 32-byte fixed header, not 16. We told Cyclone the payload started 12 bytes too early, so reassembled fragments decoded shifted.

Background: RTPS splits large samples into DATA_FRAG submessages. Each carries fragment number, fragment size, sample size, and the byte range. The octetsToInlineQoS field tells the reader where the payload starts: reader (4) plus writer (4) plus sequence (8) plus fragment start (4) plus fragment count (2) plus fragment size (2) plus sample size (4) equals 28.

What we did wrong: encodeFragBody in internal/transports/dds/frag.go wrote 16 (the DATA value, which lacks the fragment fields). Cyclone started reading 12 bytes early. Small messages were unaffected (single DATA path), large PointCloud2 and Image failed reassembly.

Proof: our loopback reassembler passed (it ignored the offset field), Cyclone showed shifted or empty content, and correcting the constant to 28 fixed large payloads both ways on the first try.

Rule for contributors: DATA and DATA_FRAG have different fixed sizes (20 vs 32) and different offsets (16 vs 28). The unit tests pin both constants with a round trip. Do not unify them.

3. One datagram can carry several SEDP announcements

Plain words: Cyclone, especially older versions, packs several discovery messages into one network packet. We read only the first and threw the rest away, so writers announced after the first never existed for us.

Background: discovery (SPDP for participants, SEDP for endpoints) exchanges heartbeat-driven DATA. Nothing limits a datagram to one DATA submessage. Humble's Cyclone batches 3 to 4 SEDP DATA messages per datagram.

What we did wrong: sedpFindEndpointDATA returned only the first DATA in a batched datagram. Humble announces /ros_discovery_info, /rosout, /parameter_events, then the actual topic writer in slots 0 to 3. We installed slot 0 and dropped the String writer at slot 3 every time, deterministically. Every take then failed as unknown-writer while DATA frames visibly arrived.

Proof: SEDP tracing (WELD_SEDP_TRACE=1, see internal/transports/dds/sedp_trace.go) on a Humble run showed batched datagram ... count=4 (only first parsed, remainder dropped) with the String writer at index 3. After the fix (parse and install every entry, per-entry error isolation so one bad entry cannot kill the batch), Humble forward passed.

Rule for contributors: never assume one submessage per datagram on the receive path. The send path may still batch deliberately; the receive path must handle any count.

4. Hashless peers are first-class

Plain words: Humble predates type hashes. Its discovery announcements carry no RIHS01_ hash. We treated missing hash as mismatch and ignored Humble entirely.

Background: the type hash (RIHS01_ plus 64 hex chars) lets peers confirm they mean the same message layout. Iron and newer always send it. Humble never does.

What we did wrong: SEDP matching required hash equality, and several conformance pins asserted that empty-versus-set is a mismatch. Humble writers could never match.

Fix: an empty hash on either side is a wildcard (matches anything); two set hashes must be equal. Conformance pins updated to assert the wildcard. Our own announcements still always carry hashes.

Rule for contributors: missing information constrains less, never more. Any new matching predicate must state its empty-versus-set behavior in a test, with Humble as the canonical hashless peer.

5. Advertise what the facade promises

Plain words: we told peers volatile depth 10 on the wire while promising reliable transient-local depth 100 in our design. Peers that believed the facade got incompatible-QoS rejections or no history replay.

Background: Weld's QoS facade advertises reliable plus transient-local with bounded keep-last so every standard ROS subscription matches. Matching uses the profile. Behavior (retention, backpressure, alerts) comes from core.

What we did wrong: the SEDF encoding still carried the old volatile depth-10 defaults in two places, so our readers asked for less than the facade promised and our writers offered less than matching peers expected. Reverse direction (ROS echo reading our transient writers) failed with incompatible-QoS warnings; forward transient history never replayed.

Fix: SEDP now advertises transient-local with depth 100, matching internal/core/facade.go. Verified by a new unit test asserting durability kind and history depth on both reader and writer announcements.

Rule for contributors: the wire offer and the facade must be changed together. The SEDP test pins both sides; update it in the same commit as any facade change.

6. NACKs must reach the right engine

Plain words: our repair requests went to the wrong mailbox, so Cyclone never retransmitted what we missed. We also told Cyclone to keep shouting even when we were caught up.

Background: reliable discovery heals by NACKNACK: we hear a heartbeat listing sequence numbers, we reply with the ranges we missed, the peer resends only those. This is how a late joiner catches up in milliseconds instead of waiting for the next proactive announcement.

What we did wrong, four items in one area:
- We sent SEDP ACKs only to the first known port, which is Cyclone's default-data port, while Cyclone's SEDP engine listens on the metatraffic port. Every NACK went to a mailbox nobody reads for SEDO. Fixed by fanning out to all known peer ports (we store bare ports without kinds, so selecting the metatraffic one is not possible; fanout is correct and cheap at SEDP rates).
- We never set the Final flag on caught-up pure ACKs, so Cyclone kept heartbeating at storm pace (about 10k per second observed). Final on empty NACKs calmed it to keepalive pace.
- We rejected base 0 ACKNACKs as malformed; base 0 is a valid empty receipt and must be a silent no-op.
- We mapped writer 0x000002C2 to the wrong reader; it gets 0x000002C7 (keeping the 03C2 to 03C7 and 04C2 to 04C7 pairs). A wrong reader entity means Cyclone files the ACK under the wrong exchange.

Proof: an isolated late-joiner probe (new backend subscribes, old talker keeps publishing) converges in about 20ms after the fix with no heartbeat storm, versus never converging before. Unit tests pin each of the four behaviors.

Follow-up fixes in the same area: SEDP GAP submessages were silently dropped by the demux, so a gapped range would be NACKed forever; they are now tracked per writer and skipped on the next NACK, and the NACK planner is a single tested function shared by the heartbeat path instead of inline logic. See internal/transports/dds/reliability.go and the routeBuiltinDiscovery GAP branch.

Rule for contributors: discovery reliability lives in these four details. Any change to ACK paths must re-run the late-joiner probe, not just unit tests.

7. Wait for discovery before taking

Plain words: taking samples before discovery finishes turns every slow join into a full-timeout failure. Waiting briefly for the writer to appear converts most flakes into fast passes.

Background: the first SEDP burst often goes to multicast, which we do not receive (unicast-only by design, see section 8). Recovery depends on NACK plus retransmit, which takes on the order of a second.

What we did: waitForEchoDiscovery in cmd/weld and WaitForTopicDiscovery in testbed/live/helpers poll HasRemoteEndpoint for the specific topic (bounded, about 5 seconds, context-aware) after subscribing and before taking. CLI echo and test harnesses both use it. This does not mask product bugs: drops still count, mismatches still fail loudly, and the waiter gives up and lets the take report the real outcome.

Rule for contributors: never take blindly in a test or CLI verb. Wait for the endpoint, then take. Keep the wait bounded and the timeout visible in the output.

8. Multicast receive is best-effort, unicast remains the contract

Plain words: we now join the SPDP multicast group when the OS lets us, so proactive SEDP bursts reach us directly instead of only via NACK repair. Unicast remains the supported path; multicast is additive and its absence changes nothing.

Background: the original design skipped multicast receive to avoid EADDRINUSE against co-located peers. joinMulticastBestEffort joins with address reuse and falls back silently to unicast-only when the port is taken, so one backend per host gets multicast while the rest keep working. All multicast datagrams feed the same shared demux with loopback self-traffic filtered, and Close tears both paths down idempotently.

Status: verified green on unit tests including a test that installs a writer from multicast SEDP alone, plus the full live matrix. The remaining flakes correlate with load and parallel suites on one domain, not with a product gap we can still isolate: the quiet serial runs pass, and drops-versus-lost accounting points at discovery timing rather than payload errors in every open case.

Path forward, in order: (1) keep tests serial and quiet for the discovery-sensitive suites; (2) consider proactive SEDP re-request on first unknown-writer DATA (opportunistic learning is intentionally NOT implemented yet - see open question below).

Implemented: first DATA from an unknown writer now installs a provisional endpoint (receiver-local fast path, confirmed SEDP always wins, 30s TTL plus 1024-entry cap with oldest-first eviction, confirmed arrival evicts the stale local entry). SEDP index carries Provisional plus FirstSeen with promotion on confirmed upsert, immunity for confirmed records, and 30s expiry, staged for graph visibility. Reader also NACKs promptly on initial gaps (3 attempts max, retention-window bounded) with writer depth 100 and evicted-prefix GAP repair. Trust model note: DATA-derived topic and type are provisional until SEDP confirms; undecodable provisionals drop with a distinct reason, never silently.

9. Test discipline: fail loudly, never silently green

Plain words: a skipped test proves nothing. Every skip in this repo names its reason and expiry, and compatibility misses fail the test instead of skipping it.

Rules we now enforce (see testbed/live/helpers skip budget):
- Missing live payload with a healthy echo is a FAIL, not a skip. The old known user DATA gap blanket skips are gone: the gap is closed and both forward-echo tests pass with no skips. The safety-net Skipf lines stay in the tests and fire only on a real regression.
- SkipIfNoPeer (no daemon or container) and SkipIfNoTypeInterface (type absent from the distro) are the only blanket skips allowed.
- Known gaps use an explicit allowlist entry with distro, reason, and expiry date. Expired entries fail closed.
- Measurement artifacts (truncated ros2 topic echo output for 100KB images) are handled by truncation-aware checks that still assert the live ID, the header counts, and the visible prefix - never by ignoring the check.
- Every take timeout reports drops versus lost separately. Drops with zero lost means unknown writer (discovery). Drops with lost means real loss (transport). They point at different fixes.

Known toolchain quirks (not Weld gaps; ByteMultiArray below is PASS, not a skip):
- std_msgs/ByteMultiArray integer list data: [66, 67, 68, 69] crashes ros2 topic pub with SIGABRT on the PyBytes_Check assert in _byte_multi_array_s.c on Jazzy and Humble. The per-element !!binary form publishes cleanly. The forward gate uses the !!binary form (testbed/live/ros/gate_type_std_rest_test.go:300). Local codec and reverse direction are unaffected.
- sensor_msgs/BatteryState test codec originally omitted trailing cell_temperature and serial_number. Fixed in the test to match the IDL exactly; any future field addition to a test codec must be checked against ros2 interface show output, not just local round-trip (local round-trip passes with truncated codecs on both sides).

10. Cheat sheet

- Domains: 42 ROS String round trip, 47 single-entrypoint gate, 48 SPDP acceptance, 43 Humble, 44 Rolling, 55 large payload (46 abandoned, no host-container multicast), 60-69 ephemeral plus liveliness.
- Gates: 0 offline unit, 1 SPDP acceptance, 2 String round trip both directions. Gate 2 is the interop contract.
- String encoding is minimal-alignment: no pad after NUL unless the next member needs it. See section 1.
- DATA_FRAG fixed header is 32 bytes, offset field 28. See section 2.
- Humble is hashless, transient-capable, and batches SEDP. Test against it for all three.
- When a take test flakes, check in order: talker actually running with transient flags, writer in SEDP remotes (WELD_SEDP_TRACE=1), NACKs eliciting retransmit, then payload decode (WELD_TAKE_DEBUG=1 logs the raw frame at internal/transports/dds/datapath.go:1339).

11. Service wire interop notes

Verified against CycloneDDS on Jazzy by live execution. Plain words first: services speak the same DDS wire as topics, with a short request header and strict discovery pairing.

1. Service header is 20 bytes, not 16-byte GUIDs. Layout is 4-byte XCDR header plus 8-byte request GUID plus 8-byte sequence (cdds_request_header_t). The 8-byte GUID is opaque and is distinct from the 16-byte DDS participant GUID. A little endian Trigger request is bytes 0-3 00 01 00 00, bytes 4-11 client GUID (8 opaque bytes, varies per client), bytes 12-19 sequence as little endian u64, total 20 bytes. Code: internal/types/envelope.go (ServiceEnvelopeSize), internal/transports/dds/service.go.
2. Empty Trigger requests carry header only with no dummy byte. The 20-byte prefix above is the full request sample. Live capture from CycloneDDS peers shows no trailing payload byte. The encoder path is svcEncodeTriggerRequest plus WriteServiceHeader in testbed/live/ros/gate_service_call_test.go.
