4. Service endpoints use volatile durability. DeclareServiceEndpoint sets VolatileDurability: true and sedpEncodeDurabilityFor announces volatile kind 0, while normal topic endpoints announce transient-local. This matches ROS service writers, which offer volatile. Code: internal/transports/dds/service.go, internal/transports/dds/sedp.go.
5. Dot-format serviceid and clientid USER_DATA pairing is required by rmw availability checks. Each service endpoint announces serviceid as 16 bytes rendered as dot-separated lowercase hex pairs (12-byte participant prefix plus 4-byte name hash, for example 1.10.9a.9c.bc.6.94.20.76.2d.69.93.0.0.0.8). Request reader plus reply writer for one service share the ID. CheckServiceAvailable requires nonzero request readers plus reply writers, then matches the client want ID against advertised IDs with fallback to nonempty sets only when both ID lists are empty. Code: serviceIDForName, CheckServiceAvailable, ParseServiceUserdata in internal/transports/dds/service.go.
6. SEDP DATA uses per-writer sequence streams. A prior shared process-wide counter gave every builtin writer stream gaps (1, 3, 5 interleaved across the 0x000003C2 and 0x000004C2 writers). Reliable CycloneDDS readers held the out-of-order samples while Weld never repairs SEDP, so endpoints randomly never appeared. The fix keys sequence state on participant prefix plus writer entity (sedpWriterSeqLast), and announceWithRoutes skips sends while routes are empty so no sequence is consumed by a dropped send. Code: internal/transports/dds/sedp.go, internal/transports/dds/backend.go.
7. Current status. Forward Trigger PASS, reverse Trigger PASS, Trigger deadline PASS; all 3 params gates PASS single-backend (Get, atomic Set, validation reject); all 4 large-payload gates PASS on domain 55 (ImageFragmented, PointCloud2Fragmented, SensorImageLarge, SensorPointCloud2Large), no skips. Domain 46 abandoned for host-container traffic. Reverse availability quirk resolved via synchronous SEDP announce.

12. Extended wire coverage: keyed topics, durability detail, partitions, per-profile writers, content filters, loaned delivery, liveliness enforcement, security posture

Verified against CycloneDDS by live execution plus unit pins. Plain words first: the eight items below close the gap between basic topic interop and a complete DDS citizen. Each carries its honest boundary; nothing here is faked.

1. Keyed topics. Key bytes extract from XCDR via introspection IsKey members, instance handles are truncated SHA-256 over type plus key, and keyed versus unkeyed mismatch is a loud incompatible at match time, never a silent filter drop. With-key entity kinds (writer 0x02, reader 0x07) announce and round-trip. Boundary: keyed DATA from a vendor writer whose type has no registered descriptor is dropped loudly with a named keyed-decode reason instead of delivering unkeyed; standard ROS types are unkeyed, so this path activates only for keyed IDLs. Code: internal/transports/dds/keyed.go, internal/types/keydefs.go. Gate: testbed/live/ros/gate_keyed_live_test.go.
2. Durability service plus depth. The 28-byte DURABILITY_SERVICE value (PID 0x001e) encodes and parses with Cyclone layout and golden bytes, and every SEDP announce carries Weld caps (keep-last 100). Depth normalizes per vendor (Cyclone 1, core 10, facade 100, Zenoh 42) without silently reducing an explicit request, and late-joiner replay is bounded by writer depth and caps. Live proof runs both directions: a late Weld reader replays every sample at least once from a Cyclone transient writer, and a late Cyclone echo replays every Weld backlog sample at least once (presence, duplicates allowed as transport-correct at-least-once repair). The core matcher truth table is deliberately untouched: durability-service mismatch is not an RxO error there, matching the never-check-history rule. Code: internal/transports/dds/durability.go. Gates: testbed/live/ros/gate_durability_live_test.go.
3. Partitions. Names validate (printable ASCII except comma, bounded count and length), matching follows DDS wildcard semantics with empty meaning the default partition, and a missing common partition is a quiet non-match, never an RxO error. Endpoints carry partitions, announce them, and the match gate enforces partition-aware pairing. Boundary: SPDP route tables stay per-participant (partitions do not change locators); isolation is enforced at SEDP match time. Code: internal/transports/dds/partition.go. Gate: testbed/live/ros/gate_partition_live_test.go.
4. Per-profile writer QoS. ForwardQoS translates a resolved best-available profile to exact SEDP wire values (reliability, durability, history, deadline, lifespan, liveliness) with typed loud refusal naming topic, endpoint pair, and policy kind instead of silent downgrade; ReverseQoS mirrors the FastDDS shape back. Declared endpoints may carry a translated WireOffer; absent it the facade announce stays byte-identical, so all existing gates are unaffected. Boundary: the backend still announces facade by default; per-profile announce requires the explicit WithQoS declare path. Code: internal/transports/dds/writerqos.go. Gate: testbed/live/ros/gate_writerqos_live_test.go.
5. Content-filtered topics. A documented SQL subset (comparisons, BETWEEN, IN, LIKE, AND/OR/NOT, nested paths) compiles once and evaluates per sample against XCDR via type descriptors, with distinct miss versus eval-error drop reasons. The take path filters server-side; unregistered topics deliver unfiltered. Code: internal/transports/dds/contentfilter.go, takehooks.go. Gate: testbed/live/ros/gate_contentfilter_live_test.go.
6. Loaned zero-copy delivery. A bounded loan pool with consume-on-return handles plus truthful can-loan negotiation: true only for self-contained memcpy-safe types over the in-process path, false with a named reason everywhere else including all cross-vendor pairs. The anti-cheat core is that a true answer is never given where a copy actually happens. Boundary: no shared-memory transport exists, so cross-process and cross-vendor delivery always copies. Code: internal/transports/dds/loan.go, sdk/topic.go. Gate: testbed/live/ros/gate_loan_live_test.go.
7. Deadline plus liveliness enforcement. Trackers carry alive, stale (1x lease), and expired (2x lease) states with hysteresis and clock injection; delivery gating is pure (expired drops loudly naming lease duration, stale holds uncounted, deadline misses drop naming the policy) while the old monitoring Check semantics are unchanged. The backend wires trackers on explicit SubscribeWithQoS only; default Subscribe keeps unwatched behavior exactly. The matcher truth table is untouched. Code: internal/core/liveliness.go. Gate: testbed/live/ros/gate_liveliness_live_test.go.
8. Security posture. Security PIDs parse and tolerate (never kill discovery), secured peers classify as compatible-unknown or requires-crypto, and protected submessages drop loudly under a distinct reason instead of attempting decryption theater. An explicit gap block names what is NOT implemented: auth handshake, crypto transforms, access control, revocation. Boundary: this is detection plus loud refusal, not DDS Security. Code: internal/transports/dds/security.go. Gate: testbed/live/ros/gate_security_live_test.go.
9. NACK hardening found along the way. Three real bugs fixed while wiring the above, each proven live: user NACKs now carry the real local reader entity (was EntityUnknown, which Cyclone disregards), an INFO_DST naming the writer participant (without it Cyclone logs not a connection), and no Final flag on repair requests (Final means no response needed). NACK bases derive from contiguous received state, not highest seen, so late joiners request from 1 instead of skipping the gap. Heartbeat NACKs carry explicit missing-bit bitmaps (base-only NACKs draw heartbeats while bitmap NACKs queue retransmit on Cyclone peers). A silent-writer heartbeat loop advertises retained ranges every second so late joiners learn backlog exists. Repair serves pace 2ms per packet so a full-backlog burst does not overflow the peer socket buffer (observed live: unpaced 13-packet bursts losing most packets while the peer still advanced its base). The reverse gate pins reliable on the ros2 echo (transient echo defaults to best-effort and never NACKs) and settles after publishing so the echo NACKs from a stable base.
